1
00:00:00,090 --> 00:00:01,380
<u>System Design Fundamentals</u>

2
00:00:02,450 --> 00:00:03,520
<i>What is system design?</i>

3
00:00:04,762 --> 00:00:10,582
System design describes how a complete product works across clients, APIs, services, storage, caches, queues, workers,

4
00:00:10,712 --> 00:00:11,632
and external systems.

5
00:00:12,332 --> 00:00:16,852
For a mobile engineer, the key shift is from code inside the app to the behavior of the whole distributed system.

6
00:00:18,364 --> 00:00:20,944
<i>How is system design different from application architecture?</i>

7
00:00:22,462 --> 00:00:25,372
Application architecture organizes code inside one application.

8
00:00:26,082 --> 00:00:29,522
System design follows responsibilities and data across multiple components.

9
00:00:30,222 --> 00:00:34,482
In application architecture, the flow usually starts at the user interface, then moves through the ViewModel,

10
00:00:34,642 --> 00:00:37,442
a use case, and a repository to local or remote data sources.

11
00:00:38,152 --> 00:00:43,092
System design looks at a wider scope: the mobile client communicates with APIs, and the system also includes services,

12
00:00:43,242 --> 00:00:45,412
databases, caches, queues, and workers.

13
00:00:46,926 --> 00:00:48,086
<i>What is a system boundary?</i>

14
00:00:49,627 --> 00:00:53,337
A system boundary defines what the design owns and what it treats as an external dependency.

15
00:00:54,047 --> 00:00:58,327
Clear boundaries make responsibilities, contracts, trust zones, and failure ownership explicit.

16
00:00:59,849 --> 00:01:01,239
<i>What are functional requirements?</i>

17
00:01:02,747 --> 00:01:07,757
Functional requirements describe what users or other systems must be able to do, such as upload media, send a message,

18
00:01:07,897 --> 00:01:10,057
edit data offline, or receive notifications.

19
00:01:11,937 --> 00:01:13,557
<i>What are non-functional requirements?</i>

20
00:01:14,810 --> 00:01:19,850
Non-functional requirements describe qualities and constraints such as latency, throughput, availability, durability,

21
00:01:19,960 --> 00:01:22,480
consistency, security, cost, and operability.

22
00:01:23,977 --> 00:01:26,047
<i>Why should assumptions and constraints be explicit?</i>

23
00:01:27,566 --> 00:01:33,306
Traffic, data volume, regions, device connectivity, budget, platform limits, and regulatory rules can change the design.

24
00:01:34,016 --> 00:01:37,446
Hidden assumptions make an architecture look correct only until real conditions differ.

25
00:01:38,969 --> 00:01:40,549
<i>What is a component responsibility?</i>

26
00:01:42,078 --> 00:01:45,048
A component responsibility is the specific job a component owns.

27
00:01:45,758 --> 00:01:50,448
Good boundaries reduce overlap and make it clearer which component owns data, validation, persistence, retries,

28
00:01:50,628 --> 00:01:51,758
or external communication.

29
00:01:53,289 --> 00:01:55,619
<i>What is an interface or contract between components?</i>

30
00:01:57,147 --> 00:02:00,247
A contract defines how components interact and what each side can rely on.

31
00:02:00,937 --> 00:02:06,457
It may be an API, event schema, database boundary, or protocol and should include success, validation, compatibility,

32
00:02:06,637 --> 00:02:07,567
and failure semantics.

33
00:02:09,102 --> 00:02:10,242
<i>What is data ownership?</i>

34
00:02:11,779 --> 00:02:15,679
Data ownership means identifying which component is authoritative for changing a piece of state.

35
00:02:16,379 --> 00:02:20,569
Other components may cache or replicate it, but they should not silently become competing authorities.

36
00:02:22,433 --> 00:02:23,523
<i>What is a source of truth?</i>

37
00:02:24,778 --> 00:02:28,268
A source of truth is the primary owner from which a particular view of state is derived.

38
00:02:28,968 --> 00:02:29,918
The exact scope matters:

39
00:02:30,098 --> 00:02:35,338
a local database can be the source of truth for mobile UI presentation while the backend remains authoritative for shared domain state.

40
00:02:36,849 --> 00:02:37,699
<i>What is latency?</i>

41
00:02:39,219 --> 00:02:42,249
Latency is the time one operation takes from the perspective being measured.

42
00:02:42,949 --> 00:02:48,419
Client-perceived latency may include radio wakeup, DNS/TLS, network transfer, server processing, retries,

43
00:02:48,569 --> 00:02:49,299
and local rendering.

44
00:02:50,838 --> 00:02:51,678
<i>What is throughput?</i>

45
00:02:53,203 --> 00:02:58,013
Throughput is the amount of work completed per unit of time, for example requests per second, messages per second,

46
00:02:58,173 --> 00:02:59,343
or jobs processed per minute.

47
00:03:00,053 --> 00:03:02,063
High throughput does not guarantee low latency.

48
00:03:03,574 --> 00:03:05,474
<i>What is the basic system design process?</i>

49
00:03:07,019 --> 00:03:11,489
Start with requirements, then constraints and assumptions, high-level architecture, data model and APIs,

50
00:03:11,639 --> 00:03:14,839
critical data flows, scaling strategy, failure handling, and trade-offs.

51
00:03:15,539 --> 00:03:19,189
The process is iterative because discoveries in one step can change earlier decisions.
